Your phone has a photo album for the neighborhood history
walk. The spreadsheet export contains captions and routes;
the catalog app exports a JSON list that includes whether
each photo may be shared. You want a short review copy, not
a new master catalog. In both files, the first job is to
trust the format's rules rather than what seems to line up
on the screen.

\begin{objectives}
\item Explain why a quoted comma is not a CSV field separator.
\item Recognize JSON objects, arrays and scalar types and inspect them with jq.
\item Export selected data through a format-aware reader without altering its source.
\end{objectives}
\IndexTerm{CSV}
\IndexTerm{JSON}
\IndexCommand{jq}

\section{When a comma is part of a field}
Use a new practice directory and a portable \cmd|sh| session.
Run the first two commands separately and stop if either fails.
The data is fictional and contains no images, locations of
private homes or personal contact information.

\begin{terminal}
practice=$(mktemp -d /tmp/utp24.XXXXXX)
cd "$practice"
printf '%s\n' 'id,caption,route' \
  '001,"Bridge, east side",River' \
  '002,"The ""Old"" Gate",Hill' > photos.csv
\end{terminal}

CSV is short for comma-separated values. On the first line,
all the commas really are separators. On the second, the
comma after \texttt{Bridge} is part of the caption because
the caption is enclosed in double quotes. The doubled quote
inside the third caption represents one literal quote.
These quote marks are part of the file, not the single
quotes that protect an argument while it passes through the
shell.

\begin{terminal}
cut -d ',' -f 2 photos.csv
\end{terminal}
\begin{terminaloutput}
caption
"Bridge
"The ""Old"" Gate"
\end{terminaloutput}

This output is the demonstration of a wrong tool choice,
not a useful caption list. The first data caption has been
truncated at its own comma. The second has retained its
CSV quoting rather than returning the decoded caption.
The command has done exactly what it was told:
\texttt{-d} specifies one literal delimiter and asks no
questions about its context. Many errors of this kind do
not generate a diagnostic. A successful exit status just
says that the wrong operation completed.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-24/csv.tikz}
\caption{The CSV reader crosses the quoted comma without starting a new field.}
\label{fig:24-csv}
\VisualDescription{The row breaks into the ID 001, the caption Bridge, east side, and the route River. The comma inside the caption stays in that field.}
\end{figure}

CSV files are not all identical. An exporter can differ in
encoding, line endings, separator, header conventions and
the escape rules for quotation marks. A quoted field may
include a newline, so a physical line count does not always
equal a count of records. Ask which settings exported the
file and preview the parsed data. Don't split, remove lines,
or count fields with simple text tools until you know that
the particular input really allows it. A small file can
run into the same problem as a million-row export.

\section{Read CSV with a CSV reader}
This optional step uses Python 3's standard \texttt{csv}
module; it is not an assumption about the Unix base system.
Check availability with \cmd|command -v python3|. If unavailable,
inspect the fixture above and skip the Python example; a
spreadsheet importer with the right CSV options is another
way to verify the two captions. There is no need to install
anything just to see the distinction.

\begin{terminal}
python3 - <<'PY'
import csv
with open("photos.csv", encoding="utf-8", newline="") as source:
    for row in csv.DictReader(source):
        print(row["id"], row["caption"], sep=": ")
PY
\end{terminal}
\begin{terminaloutput}
001: Bridge, east side
002: The "Old" Gate
\end{terminaloutput}

\cmd|python3 -| reads the little program from standard input.
The shell's \cmd|<<'PY'| supplies the following lines until
the line containing only \texttt{PY}; the quoted marker tells
the shell not to expand the program's contents. The indentation
is part of the program. Inside, \texttt{csv.DictReader} uses
the first record as field names, and each \texttt{row} supplies
the fields by name. The open settings specify UTF-8 and let
the CSV reader handle newlines. No input file is opened for
writing. The display inserts \texttt{: } between the selected
fields for humans; it does not claim to be another CSV file.

\begin{quicktable}{M{1.23in}Y}
\toprule
Input trait & Why it changes the recipe \\
\midrule
Comma in quoted field & A reader must track quotations before assigning field positions. \\
Doubled quote & The decoded field contains one quote, not two. \\
Quoted newline & A record can occupy multiple physical lines. \\
Leading zeros & \texttt{001} is an identifier here, not a quantity to convert to a number. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\section{Look at the shape of JSON}
JSON records have names and types rather than a fixed column
number. The practical question changes from ``which column
contains the caption?'' to ``which object or array holds the
caption, and what kind of value is it?'' Create the practice
catalog using the same quoted marker technique as above, this
time as input to \cmd|cat|. The closing marker goes on a line
by itself, without indenting it.

\begin{terminal}
cat > photos.json <<'JSON'
[
  {"id":"001","caption":"Bridge, east side","share":true,"score":7},
  {"id":"002","caption":"The \"Old\" Gate","share":false,"score":null}
]
JSON
\end{terminal}

The outer square brackets enclose an \FirstUse{array}: an
ordered list of values. Each pair of braces encloses an
\FirstUse{object}: named members and their values. A quoted
name, such as \texttt{"score"}, is followed by a colon and
then a value. Here the first score is the number 7, and
the second is an explicit \texttt{null}. \texttt{true} and
\texttt{false} are Boolean values, not quoted words. The
identifier \texttt{"001"} is a string because leading zeros
matter. JSON uses a backslash to protect an interior quote
in a string, not CSV's doubled quote.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-24/json.tikz}
\caption{Inspect the outer value before deciding how to reach a field.}
\label{fig:24-json}
\VisualDescription{An array contains two objects; the first object has ID 001 and Boolean true for share, and the second has ID 002 and Boolean false.}
\end{figure}

\begin{quicktable}{M{1.26in}Y}
\toprule
JSON value & How to read it in this catalog \\
\midrule
\texttt{"001"} & A string of three characters, retaining leading zeros. \\
\texttt{7} & A number; the number is not the quoted string \texttt{"7"}. \\
\texttt{true}, \texttt{false} & Boolean decisions; \texttt{"false"} is merely a nonempty string. \\
\texttt{null} & An explicit null value, not an empty string and not necessarily a missing field. \\
\texttt{[\,]}, \texttt{\{\,\}} & Delimiters for an array and an object, respectively. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\section{Ask jq for values, not lines}
\label{sec:24-jq}
\cmd|jq| is an optional JSON utility available separately
on Linux, macOS and the BSD systems. Run
\cmd|command -v jq| to check. If it is absent, read the
fixture and follow the explanations without running the
jq examples. A local package manager and the upstream
documentation can help when you decide you need it.

\begin{terminal}
jq '.' photos.json
jq 'type' photos.json
\end{terminal}

The dot is an identity filter: it receives and emits the
same JSON value, though the display may be formatted
differently. The second filter emits the JSON string
\texttt{"array"}. For this single document, an identity
pass is a useful parse check, but \cmd|jq| can accept a
stream of multiple whitespace-separated JSON values; success
alone does not prove that an arbitrary input contains
exactly one catalog.

\begin{terminal}
jq '.[0].caption' photos.json
jq -r '.[] | .caption' photos.json
\end{terminal}

Array indexing starts at zero. The first filter reaches
the first object, then its caption, and jq prints it as
a quoted JSON string. The second filter uses \cmd|.[]|
to emit each array member. The bar inside the quoted
program is jq's pipeline, not the shell's. Each object
goes to \cmd|.caption|, yielding each caption. The optional
\cmd|-r| flag sends string values as raw text, without JSON
string syntax. That display is fine for reading two short
captions, but raw output isn't lossless as a line-oriented
export: a caption containing a newline can look like
two records. Keep JSON syntax if a recipient will parse JSON.

\begin{terminal}
jq -r '.[] | select(.share == true) | .id' photos.json
\end{terminal}

\noindent
Expect exactly \texttt{001}. \cmd|select| passes along an
object only when its test is true, so the output includes
neither the withheld \texttt{002} nor any unrelated fields.
The test is expressly against a Boolean. A sloppy rule that
checks only for a truthy value can accept a nonempty string
\texttt{"false"}; jq treats only \texttt{false} and
\texttt{null} as false in conditions. Even this stricter
rule isn't a substitute for asking what sharing rights an
app export actually represents. Reading values accurately
does not grant permissions.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-24/filter.tikz}
\caption{Expand the array, filter on a Boolean, then project the identifier.}
\label{fig:24-filter}
\VisualDescription{Both photo objects enter the selection; only the true share flag passes, and the string 001 is the projected result.}
\end{figure}

\begin{dialoguebox}{These values look the same}
\Brian{If a catalog says ``false'' with quotes, surely the picture is still private?}
\Stephen{It should stay private, but a program can mistake the nonempty string for permission.}
\Brian{Why not just replace all the quoted words before reading it?}
\Stephen{That could change captions too, and it guesses what the exporter meant.}
\Brian{Then compare against the Boolean itself and investigate unexpected types?}
\Stephen{Yes. And when anything is ambiguous, don't include the photo.}
\Brian{I can preserve the full source without treating its labels as unquestionable.}
\DialogueConclusion{Require the type your decision depends on.}
\end{dialoguebox}

\section{Save a fresh report and verify it}
This report keeps two fields and preserves a JSON array,
even if no objects qualify. It uses the same test as
before. In jq, \cmd|{id, caption}| constructs an object
containing those two named members; the outer brackets
collect the emitted objects into an array. Run the first
filter without redirection to inspect it before writing.
Then write only to the unused practice filename shown.

\begin{terminal}
jq '[.[] | select(.share == true) | {id, caption}]' photos.json
jq '[.[] | select(.share == true) | {id, caption}]' photos.json \
  > review.json
jq 'length' review.json
\end{terminal}

The array should contain one object, with the Bridge
caption and string ID \texttt{001}. The final command
reports the array's length, which is 1. A parse error
can still leave an empty or incomplete redirected file:
check the writer's status before distributing its output.
Do not type the same input and output path even when a
recipe calls the result ``cleaned.'' You may need the
full file to explain which records were withheld or to
rebuild the report under a corrected rule.

If your next step is a CSV export, use a writer that
knows CSV quotation rules. Joining a JSON array of fields
with commas by hand merely recreates the mistake at the
start of the chapter. Preview exports in their destination
application as data, not as code or formulas. A document
can contain formula-like strings that a spreadsheet treats
as active expressions during import unless you explicitly
choose an appropriate text-only import mode. Do not use
real private records to explore those settings.

\begin{furtherreading}
\ReadingItem{RFC 4180}{A description of a common CSV format and its quotation rules}{https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4180}
\ReadingItem{RFC 8259}{JSON syntax and value types}{https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8259}
\ReadingItem{Python csv documentation}{Dialects, newline handling and named columns}{https://docs.python.org/3/library/csv.html}
\ReadingItem{jq manual}{Filters, raw output and types}{https://jqlang.github.io/jq/manual/}
\end{furtherreading}

\begin{takeaways}
\item Choose a reader that understands the format, not just its visible separator.
\item Keep quoted strings distinct from numeric and Boolean values.
\item Preview, write to a new file and check what the reader of that file will see.
\end{takeaways}

\begin{exercise}{Find the correct route}
\label{ex:24-route}
In the optional Python example, print the \texttt{route}
instead of the \texttt{caption}. Expect \texttt{001: River}
then \texttt{002: Hill}. If Python is absent, identify
those same fields by tracing the CSV's quote boundaries.
No extra file is created.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
Replace \texttt{row["caption"]} in the print call with
\texttt{row["route"]}. The comma inside the Bridge caption
does not advance the field index, so the route is River.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Read the withheld ID}
\label{ex:24-withheld}
For the practice fixture only, list IDs with the Boolean
\texttt{false} share flag. Expect \texttt{002}.
Keep the output on screen; nothing extra needs cleaning up.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
jq -r '.[] | select(.share == false) | .id' photos.json
\end{terminal}
Without jq, validate by inspecting the second object in the fixture.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Distinguish null from a number}
\label{ex:24-types}
Have jq print the type of each score. Expect the two
quoted type names \texttt{"number"} and \texttt{"null"}.
If jq is absent, identify the two types from the source.
Do not write a file.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
jq '.[] | .score | type' photos.json
\end{terminal}
The program moves through each object, its score and then
the type of that score. It does not convert \texttt{null} to 0.
\end{solution}

